{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016kWTg1Ab3uMxJV5ATvYhxW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'All three claims fully verified with repositioned framing. Contribution\n2\t  reads as clean empirical science: (1) ordinal depth estimation as new task formulation,\n3\t  (2) dual dynamics discovery (level/diversity drift vs shape stationarity) validated\n4\t  across generator families, reference models, and 7B+ scale — the split is about\n5\t  what features measure, not where they come from, (3) depth-aware curation with OLMo-1B\n6\t  downstream gains significant under multi-seed CIs. Paper anchored in OOD detection\n7\t  + model collapse + data quality literatures (30+ refs). Text length confound controlled\n8\t  — surface features track depth, not length. AAAI/EMNLP accept as strong empirical\n9\t  contribution with surprising finding + immediate utility.\n10\t\n11\t  '\n12\timagined_experiments:\n13\t- name: feature_ablation_and_trend\n14\t  plan_id: plan_001\n15\t  description: 'Three-part analysis on MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator,\n16\t    Pythia-410M reference). (A) Feature ablation: reference-only vs surface-only vs\n17\t    full. (B) H1/H2 trend analysis per feature. (C) Perplexity pre-validation for\n18\t    Claim 3.\n19\t\n20\t    '\n21\t  expected_results:\n22\t    full_macro_f1: 0.8204\n23\t    surface_only_f1: 0.7628\n24\t    reference_only_f1: 0.7187\n25\t    full_vs_surface_f1_gap: 0.058\n26\t    full_vs_reference_f1_gap: 0.102\n27\t    ref_vs_surf_bootstrap_ci: '[0.706,0.733] vs [0.747,0.774]'\n28\t    h2_features: entropy_mean, ttr, hapax_ratio\n29\t    h1_features: tail_decay, entropy_std, entropy_max, entropy_min\n30\t    entropy_mean_slope_r2: 0.91\n31\t    ttr_slope_r2: 0.88\n32\t    hapax_ratio_slope_r2: 0.85\n33\t    entropy_std_slope_r2: 0.12\n34\t    tail_decay_slope_r2: 0.09\n35\t    median_ppl_by_depth:\n36\t    - 13.5\n37\t    - 4.96\n38\t    - 3.0\n39\t    - 2.07\n40\t    - 1.72\n41\t    ppl_depth_spearman: -0.97\n42\t  result_rationale: '[ACTUAL — plan-001-fix completed] Full > surface > reference\n43\t    ablation gap confirmed. Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) are stronger than\n44\t    initially expected (0.76 vs prior dream''s 0.63) because vocabulary impoverishment\n45\t    is a robust, high-signal depth cue. Reference features alone achieve 0.72 — weaker\n46\t    than surface alone, overturning the naive expectation that reference features\n47\t    dominate. But their complementary value is clear: full set (0.82) significantly\n48\t    exceeds both subsets. The key insight is that entropy_mean (reference-derived)\n49\t    is an H2/drift feature while entropy_std/max/min (also reference-derived) are\n50\t    H1/stationary — the split is about what''s measured (level vs shape), not the\n51\t    feature source. Perplexity monotonically decreases with depth (13.5→1.7) confirming\n52\t    that deeper text is progressively more predictable under the reference model —\n53\t    strong pre-validation for Claim 3''s downstream utility hypothesis.\n54\t\n55\t    '\n56\t  tables:\n57\t  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class)'\n58\t    columns:\n59\t    - Feature Set\n60\t    - Features\n61\t    - Macro-F1\n62\t    - gen-0 F1\n63\t    - gen-1 F1\n64\t    - gen-2+ F1\n65\t    - CV std\n66\t    rows:\n67\t    - - Reference-only\n68\t      - entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\n69\t      - 0.72\n70\t      - 0.88\n71\t      - 0.63\n72\t      - 0.65\n73\t      - 0.021\n74\t    - - Surface-only\n75\t      - ttr, hapax_ratio\n76\t      - 0.76\n77\t      - 0.9\n78\t      - 0.69\n79\t      - 0.7\n80\t      - 0.019\n81\t    - - Full\n82\t      - All 7 features\n83\t      - 0.82\n84\t      - 0.95\n85\t      - 0.76\n86\t      - 0.79\n87\t      - 0.013\n88\t  - name: 'Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Trend Analysis (linear regression slope per depth)'\n89\t    columns:\n90\t    - Feature\n91\t    - Slope\n92\t    - R²\n93\t    - Category\n94\t    - Interpretation\n95\t    rows:\n96\t    - - entropy_mean\n97\t      - -0.18\n98\t      - 0.91\n99\t      - H2-drift\n100\t      - Reference-model surprise decreases monotonically\n101\t    - - ttr\n102\t      - -0.024\n103\t      - 0.88\n104\t      - H2-drift\n105\t      - Lexical diversity declines with depth\n106\t    - - hapax_ratio\n107\t      - -0.019\n108\t      - 0.85\n109\t      - H2-drift\n110\t      - Singleton word ratio drops\n111\t    - - entropy_std\n112\t      - 0.003\n113\t      - 0.12\n114\t      - H1-stationary\n115\t      - Surprise variance near-constant\n116\t    - - entropy_max\n117\t      - 0.008\n118\t      - 0.15\n119\t      - H1-stationary\n120\t      - Tail surprise stable\n121\t    - - entropy_min\n122\t      - -0.005\n123\t      - 0.11\n124\t      - H1-stationary\n125\t      - Floor surprise stable\n126\t    - - tail_decay\n127\t      - 0.002\n128\t      - 0.09\n129\t      - H1-stationary\n130\t      - Distributional shape robust\n131\t  figures_description:\n132\t  - 'Fig 2a: Feature trend plots (mean ± std) across depth 0-4, showing clear H2 monotonic\n133\t    drift for entropy_mean/ttr/hapax_ratio and H1 stationarity for entropy_std/max/min/tail_decay'\n134\t  - 'Fig 2b: Perplexity by depth (median + IQR), monotonic decrease from 13.5 to 1.7'\n135\t  analysis: 'The ablation gap is central to the paper''s contribution positioning.\n136\t    The full_vs_surface gap (0.058) and full_vs_reference gap (0.102) confirm that\n137\t    both feature families carry non-redundant signal. Critically, entropy_mean is\n138\t    reference-derived but drifts (H2) — this cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary\n139\t    and is the headline finding. The H1/H2 split is clean: R²≥0.85 for drift features,\n140\t    R²≤0.15 for stationary features. This is the empirical backbone of Claim 2.\n141\t\n142\t    '\n143\t- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n144\t  plan_id: plan_002\n145\t  description: '5 generators: Pythia-2.8B, Pythia-1B, OLMo-2-0425-1B, Qwen3-1.7B-Base,\n146\t    Qwen3-0.6B-Base. 1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out\n147\t    (LOGO) CV. Pythia-410M as reference model for all.\n148\t\n149\t    '\n150\t  expected_results:\n151\t    logo_macro_f1_mean: 0.74\n152\t    logo_macro_f1_std: 0.03\n153\t    logo_gen0_f1_mean: 0.87\n154\t    logo_gen1_f1_mean: 0.68\n155\t    logo_gen2plus_f1_mean: 0.7\n156\t    in_distribution_pooled_macro_f1: 0.83\n157\t    pythia_2_8b_held_out_f1: 0.77\n158\t    pythia_1b_held_out_f1: 0.76\n159\t    olmo2_held_out_f1: 0.73\n160\t    qwen3_1_7b_held_out_f1: 0.72\n161\t    qwen3_0_6b_held_out_f1: 0.7\n162\t    leave_one_family_out_pythia_f1: 0.71\n163\t    leave_one_family_out_qwen_f1: 0.74\n164\t    total_chains: 23800\n165\t    attrition_rate_pct: 4.8\n166\t  result_rationale: 'LOGO mean 0.74 (within [0.70, 0.78] best range) reflects cross-generator\n167\t    generalization grounded in actual plan-001 performance (0.82 in-dist). The ~0.09\n168\t    drop from in-distribution to LOGO is expected: each generator has its own distributional\n169\t    signature, and training without the held-out generator forces reliance on transfer\n170\t    of the depth-drift pattern. Pythia-family generators transfer best to each other\n171\t    (0.76-0.77) due to shared pretraining distribution (The Pile). Cross-family transfer\n172\t    (Pythia→Qwen, Pythia→OLMo) drops to 0.70-0.73 because the reference model (Pythia-410M)\n173\t    has different baseline expectations for text from non-Pythia generators, adding\n174\t    noise to features. Qwen3-0.6B-Base is hardest (0.70) because its small capacity\n175\t    produces lower-quality text that drifts faster at gen-1, making the depth ordering\n176\t    harder to predict from larger-model-trained classifiers. Leave-one-family-out\n177\t    for Pythia (train on OLMo+Qwen, test on Pythia-2.8B+1B) gives 0.71, confirming\n178\t    the signal is not Pythia-specific. Attrition slightly higher (4.8%) due to Qwen\n179\t    models'' less stable generation at depth 4.\n180\t\n181\t    '\n182\t  tables:\n183\t  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation'\n184\t    columns:\n185\t    - Held-out Generator\n186\t    - Family\n187\t    - Params\n188\t    - Macro-F1\n189\t    - gen-0 F1\n190\t    - gen-1 F1\n191\t    - gen-2+ F1\n192\t    rows:\n193\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n194\t      - Pythia\n195\t      - 2.8B\n196\t      - 0.77\n197\t      - 0.9\n198\t      - 0.71\n199\t      - 0.72\n200\t    - - Pythia-1B\n201\t      - Pythia\n202\t      - 1B\n203\t      - 0.76\n204\t      - 0.89\n205\t      - 0.7\n206\t      - 0.71\n207\t    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n208\t      - OLMo\n209\t      - 1.5B\n210\t      - 0.73\n211\t      - 0.86\n212\t      - 0.67\n213\t      - 0.68\n214\t    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n215\t      - Qwen\n216\t      - 1.7B\n217\t      - 0.72\n218\t      - 0.85\n219\t      - 0.66\n220\t      - 0.67\n221\t    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n222\t      - Qwen\n223\t      - 0.6B\n224\t      - 0.7\n225\t      - 0.83\n226\t      - 0.63\n227\t      - 0.65\n228\t    - - Mean ± std\n229\t      - —\n230\t      - —\n231\t      - 0.74 ± 0.03\n232\t      - 0.87 ± 0.03\n233\t      - 0.68 ± 0.03\n234\t      - 0.70 ± 0.03\n235\t  - name: 'Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV'\n236\t    columns:\n237\t    - Generator\n238\t    - Macro-F1\n239\t    - CV std\n240\t    rows:\n241\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n242\t      - 0.84\n243\t      - 0.014\n244\t    - - Pythia-1B\n245\t      - 0.82\n246\t      - 0.016\n247\t    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n248\t      - 0.83\n249\t      - 0.015\n250\t    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n251\t      - 0.82\n252\t      - 0.017\n253\t    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n254\t      - 0.8\n255\t      - 0.02\n256\t    - - Pooled (all 5)\n257\t      - 0.83\n258\t      - 0.012\n259\t  - name: 'Table 4b: Leave-One-Family-Out'\n260\t    columns:\n261\t    - Held-out Family\n262\t    - Generators Held Out\n263\t    - Macro-F1\n264\t    rows:\n265\t    - - Pythia\n266\t      - Pythia-2.8B + Pythia-1B\n267\t      - 0.71\n268\t    - - Qwen\n269\t      - Qwen3-1.7B + Qwen3-0.6B\n270\t      - 0.74\n271\t    - - OLMo\n272\t      - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n273\t      - 0.73\n274\t  figures_description:\n275\t  - 'Fig 3a: LOGO F1 bar chart by held-out generator, showing Pythia > OLMo > Qwen\n276\t    ordering'\n277\t  - 'Fig 3b: Confusion matrix heatmap for pooled LOGO evaluation showing gen-0 well-separated,\n278\t    gen-1/gen-2+ boundary as main error source'\n279\t  analysis: 'LOGO F1=0.74 is the centerpiece of Claim 1. The ≥0.70 threshold is cleared\n280\t    with margin. Pythia-family slight advantage (0.76-0.77) is a limitation to discuss\n281\t    honestly: Pythia-410M reference model shares tokenizer/architecture with generators.\n282\t    Leave-one-family-out (0.71) still passes the 0.70 threshold, confirming depth\n283\t    signal is generation-process-intrinsic, not family-artifact. The gen-1 vs gen-2+\n284\t    boundary remains the classification bottleneck across all generators.\n285\t\n286\t    '\n287\t- name: cross_family_reference_model\n288\t  plan_id: plan_003\n289\t  description: 'Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B,\n290\t    Qwen3-0.6B-Base) on plan_002 data. Key falsification test: does entropy_mean H2\n291\t    drift persist with non-Pythia reference? If not, dual dynamics is Pythia-specific\n292\t    artifact.\n293\t\n294\t    '\n295\t  expected_results:\n296\t    pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1: 0.83\n297\t    olmo2_1b_ref_macro_f1: 0.77\n298\t    qwen3_06b_ref_macro_f1: 0.74\n299\t    entropy_mean_depth_rho_pythia: -0.72\n300\t    entropy_mean_depth_rho_olmo: -0.65\n301\t    entropy_mean_depth_rho_qwen: -0.58\n302\t    cross_ref_mean_rho: -0.65\n303\t    entropy_std_depth_rho_pythia: -0.08\n304\t    entropy_std_depth_rho_olmo: -0.11\n305\t    entropy_std_depth_rho_qwen: -0.06\n306\t    feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean: 0.79\n307\t    feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean: 0.74\n308\t    h2_drift_confirmed_all_refs: true\n309\t    h1_stationarity_confirmed_all_refs: true\n310\t    ensemble_3refs_macro_f1: 0.85\n311\t  result_rationale: 'entropy_mean ρ with depth ranges from -0.58 to -0.72 across reference\n312\t    models (mean -0.65, within [-0.75, -0.55] best range), confirming H2 drift is\n313\t    generation-intrinsic, not Pythia-specific. All three reference models detect monotonic\n314\t    entropy_mean decrease with depth because any well-trained LM assigns progressively\n315\t    higher probability (lower NLL) to deeper text — the text literally becomes more\n316\t    generic/predictable as generation recurses. Pythia-410M gives strongest signal\n317\t    (-0.72) because it was optimized for this pipeline and its 410M capacity is well-calibrated\n318\t    to detect drift (large enough to model human text, small enough to be \"surprised\"\n319\t    by generator deviations). OLMo (-0.65) and Qwen (-0.58) are noisier because their\n320\t    different pretraining distributions create different baselines. Crucially, entropy_std\n321\t    remains near-zero correlation with depth across all three models (|ρ|<0.12), confirming\n322\t    shape stationarity (H1) is also reference-model-agnostic. The dual dynamics is\n323\t    a property of the recursive generation process, not the measurement instrument.\n324\t    This is the key falsification test for Claim 2 — it passes.\n325\t\n326\t    '\n327\t  tables:\n328\t  - name: 'Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)'\n329\t    columns:\n330\t    - Reference Model\n331\t    - Family\n332\t    - Params\n333\t    - Macro-F1\n334\t    - gen-0 F1\n335\t    - gen-1 F1\n336\t    - gen-2+ F1\n337\t    rows:\n338\t    - - Pythia-410M\n339\t      - Pythia\n340\t      - 410M\n341\t      - 0.83\n342\t      - 0.94\n343\t      - 0.77\n344\t      - 0.79\n345\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n346\t      - OLMo\n347\t      - 1B\n348\t      - 0.77\n349\t      - 0.89\n350\t      - 0.7\n351\t      - 0.72\n352\t    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n353\t      - Qwen\n354\t      - 0.6B\n355\t      - 0.74\n356\t      - 0.86\n357\t      - 0.67\n358\t      - 0.69\n359\t    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n360\t      - Mixed\n361\t      - —\n362\t      - 0.85\n363\t      - 0.95\n364\t      - 0.79\n365\t      - 0.81\n366\t  - name: 'Table 6: Entropy Feature Depth Correlation (Spearman ρ) by Reference Model'\n367\t    columns:\n368\t    - Feature\n369\t    - Pythia-410M\n370\t    - OLMo-2-1B\n371\t    - Qwen3-0.6B\n372\t    - H1/H2 Classification\n373\t    rows:\n374\t    - - entropy_mean\n375\t      - -0.72\n376\t      - -0.65\n377\t      - -0.58\n378\t      - H2-drift\n379\t    - - entropy_std\n380\t      - -0.08\n381\t      - -0.11\n382\t      - -0.06\n383\t      - H1-stationary\n384\t    - - entropy_max\n385\t      - 0.05\n386\t      - 0.03\n387\t      - 0.07\n388\t      - H1-stationary\n389\t    - - entropy_min\n390\t      - -0.1\n391\t      - -0.07\n392\t      - -0.09\n393\t      - H1-stationary\n394\t    - - tail_decay\n395\t      - 0.04\n396\t      - 0.06\n397\t      - 0.03\n398\t      - H1-stationary\n399\t  - name: 'Table 6b: Cross-Reference Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ)'\n400\t    columns:\n401\t    - Feature\n402\t    - Pythia↔OLMo\n403\t    - Pythia↔Qwen\n404\t    - OLMo↔Qwen\n405\t    rows:\n406\t    - - entropy_mean\n407\t      - 0.79\n408\t      - 0.74\n409\t      - 0.77\n410\t    - - entropy_std\n411\t      - 0.76\n412\t      - 0.71\n413\t      - 0.73\n414\t    - - tail_decay\n415\t      - 0.68\n416\t      - 0.63\n417\t      - 0.66\n418\t  figures_description:\n419\t  - 'Fig 4: entropy_mean vs depth scatter with regression lines for all 3 reference\n420\t    models — parallel downward slopes confirm H2 drift is reference-model-agnostic'\n421\t  - 'Fig 4b: entropy_std vs depth scatter — flat lines for all 3 reference models\n422\t    confirm H1 stationarity'\n423\t  analysis: 'This experiment eliminates the most dangerous threat to the paper: that\n424\t    dual dynamics is a Pythia artifact. With all three reference models showing consistent\n425\t    H2 drift for entropy_mean (ρ range -0.58 to -0.72) and H1 stationarity for shape\n426\t    features (|ρ|<0.12), the level/shape framework is confirmed as generation-intrinsic.\n427\t    The 3-ref ensemble (0.85) exceeding any single ref confirms that diverse reference\n428\t    perspectives capture complementary drift information. For the paper, Table 6 is\n429\t    the most important evidence for the \"what it measures, not where it comes from\"\n430\t    argument.\n431\t\n432\t    '\n433\t- name: downstream_utility\n434\t  plan_id: plan_004\n435\t  description: 'Fine-tune OLMo-1B (avoiding Pythia-410M circularity) on depth-stratified\n436\t    subsets. ≥3 random seeds with bootstrap CIs. Compare depth-aware filtering vs\n437\t    FastDetectGPT and LD-Score binary baselines. Sample-count-matched comparison to\n438\t    control data size confound.\n439\t\n440\t    '\n441\t  expected_results:\n442\t    downstream_model: OLMo-1B\n443\t    n_seeds: 5\n444\t    ppl_gen0_only_mean: 31.2\n445\t    ppl_gen0_only_ci:\n446\t    - 30.4\n447\t    - 32.0\n448\t    ppl_gen1_only_mean: 33.8\n449\t    ppl_gen1_only_ci:\n450\t    - 32.9\n451\t    - 34.7\n452\t    ppl_gen2_only_mean: 38.5\n453\t    ppl_gen2_only_ci:\n454\t    - 37.2\n455\t    - 39.8\n456\t    ppl_gen3_only_mean: 43.1\n457\t    ppl_gen3_only_ci:\n458\t    - 41.5\n459\t    - 44.7\n460\t    ppl_gen4_only_mean: 47.6\n461\t    ppl_gen4_only_ci:\n462\t    - 45.8\n463\t    - 49.4\n464\t    depth_quality_spearman: 0.74\n465\t    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_mean: 32.1\n466\t    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_ci:\n467\t    - 31.3\n468\t    - 32.9\n469\t    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_mean: 36.8\n470\t    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_ci:\n471\t    - 35.6\n472\t    - 38.0\n473\t    ppl_binary_ldscore_mean: 37.2\n474\t    ppl_binary_ldscore_ci:\n475\t    - 35.9\n476\t    - 38.5\n477\t    ppl_no_filter_mean: 40.1\n478\t    depth_filter_vs_binary_improvement_pct: 13.2\n479\t    data_retained_depth_filter_pct: 44\n480\t    data_retained_binary_pct: 30\n481\t    ppl_size_matched_depth_filter: 33.0\n482\t    ppl_size_matched_binary: 37.0\n483\t    size_matched_ci_non_overlapping: true\n484\t  result_rationale: 'OLMo-1B perplexity increases monotonically with training depth\n485\t    (31.2→47.6) because deeper text has compounded distributional drift from human\n486\t    text — fine-tuning on it shifts OLMo-1B''s distribution away from the human test\n487\t    set. The absolute PPL values are higher than the previous dream (which used Pythia-410M)\n488\t    because OLMo-1B is a stronger model with higher baseline expectations. Spearman\n489\t    ρ=0.74 (within [0.65, 0.85]) reflects individual sample variance within depth\n490\t    classes. Depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0+gen-1, PPL=32.1) outperforms binary\n491\t    baselines (~36.8-37.2) by 13.2% (within [8, 20] range) because binary detectors\n492\t    discard all synthetic text — including useful gen-1 content that is distributionally\n493\t    near-human. Binary filters retain only ~30% of data (mostly gen-0) while depth\n494\t    filtering retains 44% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing more and better training data.\n495\t    The size-matched comparison is critical: even when subsampling depth-filtered\n496\t    data to match binary filter count, depth filtering still wins (33.0 vs 37.0, CIs\n497\t    non-overlapping) — confirming the advantage is data quality, not quantity. Using\n498\t    OLMo-1B eliminates the Pythia reference model circularity concern. 5-seed bootstrap\n499\t    CIs are tight enough to establish significance.\n500\t\n501\t    '\n502\t  tables:\n503\t  - name: 'Table 7: Downstream Perplexity by Training Data Depth (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n504\t    columns:\n505\t    - Training Data\n506\t    - PPL Mean\n507\t    - 95% CI\n508\t    - Samples\n509\t    rows:\n510\t    - - gen-0 only\n511\t      - 31.2\n512\t      - '[30.4, 32.0]'\n513\t      - ~4800\n514\t    - - gen-1 only\n515\t      - 33.8\n516\t      - '[32.9, 34.7]'\n517\t      - ~4700\n518\t    - - gen-2 only\n519\t      - 38.5\n520\t      - '[37.2, 39.8]'\n521\t      - ~4400\n522\t    - - gen-3 only\n523\t      - 43.1\n524\t      - '[41.5, 44.7]'\n525\t      - ~4100\n526\t    - - gen-4 only\n527\t      - 47.6\n528\t      - '[45.8, 49.4]'\n529\t      - ~3800\n530\t  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n531\t    columns:\n532\t    - Method\n533\t    - PPL Mean\n534\t    - 95% CI\n535\t    - Data Retained\n536\t    - Δ vs No-Filter\n537\t    rows:\n538\t    - - Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\n539\t      - 32.1\n540\t      - '[31.3, 32.9]'\n541\t      - 44%\n542\t      - -20.0%\n543\t    - - FastDetectGPT binary\n544\t      - 36.8\n545\t      - '[35.6, 38.0]'\n546\t      - 30%\n547\t      - -8.2%\n548\t    - - LD-Score binary\n549\t      - 37.2\n550\t      - '[35.9, 38.5]'\n551\t      - 28%\n552\t      - -7.2%\n553\t    - - No filtering\n554\t      - 40.1\n555\t      - '[38.8, 41.4]'\n556\t      - 100%\n557\t      - —\n558\t  - name: 'Table 8b: Size-Matched Comparison (subsample to equal data count)'\n559\t    columns:\n560\t    - Method\n561\t    - Matched Count\n562\t    - PPL Mean\n563\t    - 95% CI\n564\t    rows:\n565\t    - - Depth-filtered (subsampled)\n566\t      - ~5500\n567\t      - 33.0\n568\t      - '[32.1, 33.9]'\n569\t    - - Binary-filtered\n570\t      - ~5500\n571\t      - 37.0\n572\t      - '[35.8, 38.2]'\n573\t    - - CIs non-overlapping\n574\t      - —\n575\t      - 'YES'\n576\t      - —\n577\t  figures_description:\n578\t  - 'Fig 5a: Perplexity vs training depth curve with error bars (5 seeds), monotonically\n579\t    increasing'\n580\t  - 'Fig 5b: Bar chart comparing filtering methods with 95% CI error bars, showing\n581\t    depth-aware < binary < no-filter'\n582\t  analysis: 'This is the practical punchline. The 13.2% improvement with CIs non-overlapping\n583\t    under size-matching establishes that depth-aware filtering provides genuine data\n584\t    quality advantage, not just a data quantity effect. gen-1 text is \"good enough\"\n585\t    for training (PPL 33.8 vs gen-0''s 31.2 — 8% gap), but gen-2+ is harmful (PPL\n586\t    38.5+, >23% worse than gen-0). Binary detectors force an all-or-nothing choice\n587\t    and discard useful gen-1 content. This directly validates Claim 3 under the strengthened\n588\t    protocol (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds, CIs, size-matched).\n589\t\n590\t    '\n591\t- name: prompt_type_comparison\n592\t  plan_id: plan_005\n593\t  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n594\t    1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths × 3 prompt types.\n595\t\n596\t    '\n597\t  expected_results:\n598\t    continuation_macro_f1: 0.82\n599\t    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.77\n600\t    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.69\n601\t    continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.17\n602\t    elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.13\n603\t    inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.08\n604\t    cross_prompt_type_macro_f1: 0.71\n605\t    prompt_drift_rate_f1_spearman: 0.93\n606\t  result_rationale: 'Continuation preserves strongest depth signal (0.82) because\n607\t    each step directly extends previous output, maximizing cumulative drift. Elaboration\n608\t    (0.77) injects new semantic direction via prompt instruction, partially resetting\n609\t    drift per step. Inspired rewriting (0.69) provides most semantic freedom — the\n610\t    model can re-anchor to its pretraining distribution at each step, yielding weakest\n611\t    depth accumulation. The strong correlation between per-depth entropy drift rate\n612\t    and F1 (ρ=0.93) confirms the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation\n613\t    → faster drift accumulation → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt transfer (0.71)\n614\t    is moderate because drift signatures are partially shared but rate differences\n615\t    create distribution shift. This result enriches the theoretical story without\n616\t    changing the core claims.\n617\t\n618\t    '\n619\t  tables:\n620\t  - name: 'Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type (Pythia-2.8B)'\n621\t    columns:\n622\t    - Prompt Type\n623\t    - Macro-F1\n624\t    - gen-0 F1\n625\t    - gen-1 F1\n626\t    - gen-2+ F1\n627\t    - Drift Rate (nats/depth)\n628\t    rows:\n629\t    - - Continuation\n630\t      - 0.82\n631\t      - 0.94\n632\t      - 0.76\n633\t      - 0.78\n634\t      - 0.17\n635\t    - - Elaboration\n636\t      - 0.77\n637\t      - 0.9\n638\t      - 0.7\n639\t      - 0.72\n640\t      - 0.13\n641\t    - - Inspired rewriting\n642\t      - 0.69\n643\t      - 0.84\n644\t      - 0.61\n645\t      - 0.63\n646\t      - 0.08\n647\t    - - Cross-prompt LOGO\n648\t      - 0.71\n649\t      - 0.85\n650\t      - 0.63\n651\t      - 0.65\n652\t      - —\n653\t  figures_description:\n654\t  - 'Fig S1: Entropy drift trajectory per prompt type, showing separation increasing\n655\t    with depth'\n656\t  analysis: 'The ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns\n657\t    with theory: semantic coupling determines drift accumulation rate. This is a supporting\n658\t    result — it enriches the paper but isn''t strictly necessary for the core claims.\n659\t    Best used to show that depth signal is graded and mechanism-consistent.\n660\t\n661\t    '\n662\t- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n663\t  plan_id: plan_006\n664\t  description: 'Parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths)\n665\t    vs continuation chains. Direct test of attractor convergence (H1) vs monotonic\n666\t    divergence (H2).\n667\t\n668\t    '\n669\t  expected_results:\n670\t    paraphrase_macro_f1_3class: 0.55\n671\t    paraphrase_gen0_f1: 0.79\n672\t    paraphrase_gen1_f1: 0.43\n673\t    paraphrase_gen2plus_f1: 0.44\n674\t    continuation_macro_f1_3class: 0.82\n675\t    paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.05\n676\t    continuation_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.48\n677\t    paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct: 64\n678\t    ks_statistic_para_vs_cont_depth3: 0.39\n679\t    ks_p_value: 1.0e-05\n680\t  result_rationale: 'Paraphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.55) because\n681\t    the semantic-preservation constraint creates attractor dynamics — distributional\n682\t    features converge after ~2 steps, consistent with ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory.\n683\t    By depth 3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.05 nats) while\n684\t    continuation drift continues (0.48 nats). The 64% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic\n685\t    chains (depth d closer to d-2 than d-1 in feature space) directly corroborates\n686\t    2-period attractor cycle predictions. The KS test (D=0.39, p<0.001) provides formal\n687\t    evidence that paraphrastic and continuation depth-3 distributions are distinct.\n688\t    This elevates the paper from \"a depth classifier\" to \"an empirical test of generation\n689\t    dynamics\" by connecting to established theoretical literature. Our H2 divergence\n690\t    for non-paraphrastic chains and H1 convergence for paraphrastic chains are two\n691\t    sides of the same coin — the presence or absence of semantic constraint determines\n692\t    whether drift accumulates or converges.\n693\t\n694\t    '\n695\t  tables:\n696\t  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Dynamics'\n697\t    columns:\n698\t    - Chain Type\n699\t    - Macro-F1\n700\t    - Entropy Drift @depth3\n701\t    - Cycle Detection %\n702\t    - KS D\n703\t    rows:\n704\t    - - Continuation\n705\t      - 0.82\n706\t      - 0.48 nats\n707\t      - <5%\n708\t      - —\n709\t    - - Paraphrasing\n710\t      - 0.55\n711\t      - 0.05 nats\n712\t      - 64%\n713\t      - —\n714\t    - - KS test (depth-3)\n715\t      - —\n716\t      - —\n717\t      - —\n718\t      - 0.39 (p<0.001)\n719\t  figures_description:\n720\t  - 'Fig 6: Dual panel — (left) entropy_mean trajectory for continuation (monotonic\n721\t    decline) vs paraphrasing (plateau after depth 2); (right) feature space PCA showing\n722\t    continuation chains diverging, paraphrastic chains oscillating'\n723\t  analysis: 'This is the conceptual anchor connecting our empirical finding to the\n724\t    attractor cycle literature. The clean separation validates why we focus on non-paraphrastic\n725\t    chains and why our approach succeeds where paraphrasing-based analysis would fail\n726\t    at depth estimation beyond ~2 classes.\n727\t\n728\t    '\n729\t- name: cross_model_chains\n730\t  plan_id: plan_007\n731\t  description: 'Cross-model chains: different generator per depth step (randomly sampled\n732\t    from 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Tests ecological validity — real contamination\n733\t    uses diverse generators.\n734\t\n735\t    '\n736\t  expected_results:\n737\t    cross_model_macro_f1: 0.71\n738\t    single_model_macro_f1_mean: 0.82\n739\t    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.88\n740\t    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.64\n741\t    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.63\n742\t    cross_vs_single_delta: -0.11\n743\t    cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.12\n744\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains lose ~11 points from single-model (0.71 vs\n745\t    0.82) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities at\n746\t    each step — the new generator''s idiosyncratic signature partially masks the accumulated\n747\t    depth drift. However, F1=0.71 still far exceeds chance (0.33) because the underlying\n748\t    drift phenomenon persists: regardless of which generator produces each step, cumulative\n749\t    deviation from human text distribution still increases with depth. gen-0 remains\n750\t    well-separated (0.88) — human text is fundamentally distinct from any generator.\n751\t    The gen-1/gen-2+ boundary degrades most (0.64/0.63) because generator-switching\n752\t    noise creates feature-space overlap between these classes. Entropy drift rate\n753\t    (0.12 nats/depth) is lower than single-model (0.17) because each generator transition\n754\t    partially resets the drift trajectory. This is the honest cost of ecological validity\n755\t    — reported transparently, it strengthens the paper.\n756\t\n757\t    '\n758\t  tables:\n759\t  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison'\n760\t    columns:\n761\t    - Chain Type\n762\t    - Macro-F1\n763\t    - gen-0 F1\n764\t    - gen-1 F1\n765\t    - gen-2+ F1\n766\t    rows:\n767\t    - - Single-model (mean of 5)\n768\t      - 0.82\n769\t      - 0.94\n770\t      - 0.76\n771\t      - 0.78\n772\t    - - Cross-model (random switch)\n773\t      - 0.71\n774\t      - 0.88\n775\t      - 0.64\n776\t      - 0.63\n777\t    - - Δ\n778\t      - -0.11\n779\t      - -0.06\n780\t      - -0.12\n781\t      - -0.15\n782\t  figures_description:\n783\t  - 'Fig S2: Feature space scatter (PCA) for single-model vs cross-model chains showing\n784\t    increased class overlap under generator switching'\n785\t  analysis: 'The 11-point drop is expected and honestly bounded. For data curation,\n786\t    the key utility is still maintained: the method separates human from synthetic\n787\t    (gen-0 F1=0.88) even under realistic multi-generator contamination. Finer depth\n788\t    estimation within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades — an honest limitation\n789\t    for the Discussion section.\n790\t\n791\t    '\n792\t- name: multi_domain_seeds\n793\t  plan_id: plan_008\n794\t  description: '4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC), creative\n795\t    (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 generators × 5 depths.\n796\t\n797\t    '\n798\t  expected_results:\n799\t    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.83\n800\t    news_macro_f1: 0.8\n801\t    scientific_macro_f1: 0.78\n802\t    creative_macro_f1: 0.74\n803\t    cross_domain_logo_f1: 0.74\n804\t    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.81\n805\t    domain_f1_variance: 0.0012\n806\t  result_rationale: 'Wikipedia performs best (0.83) as it matches the MVP development\n807\t    setting with structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to faithfully\n808\t    continue. News (0.80) has similar factual density. Scientific text (0.78) has\n809\t    specialized vocabulary that partially masks depth drift in surface features, though\n810\t    reference features still detect it. Creative writing is hardest (0.74) because\n811\t    generators produce more fluent, human-like creative text — the distributional\n812\t    gap between human and generated creative text is smaller, reducing overall drift\n813\t    magnitude. The low F1 variance across domains (0.0012) indicates depth signal\n814\t    is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Cross-domain LOGO (0.74) confirms generalization\n815\t    without domain-specific calibration. This addresses RC6 (ecological validity)\n816\t    by showing the approach works across diverse text types.\n817\t\n818\t    '\n819\t  tables:\n820\t  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification'\n821\t    columns:\n822\t    - Domain\n823\t    - Seeds\n824\t    - Macro-F1\n825\t    - gen-0 F1\n826\t    - gen-1 F1\n827\t    - gen-2+ F1\n828\t    rows:\n829\t    - - Wikipedia\n830\t      - 500\n831\t      - 0.83\n832\t      - 0.94\n833\t      - 0.77\n834\t      - 0.79\n835\t    - - News\n836\t      - 500\n837\t      - 0.8\n838\t      - 0.92\n839\t      - 0.74\n840\t      - 0.76\n841\t    - - Scientific\n842\t      - 500\n843\t      - 0.78\n844\t      - 0.9\n845\t      - 0.71\n846\t      - 0.73\n847\t    - - Creative\n848\t      - 500\n849\t      - 0.74\n850\t      - 0.86\n851\t      - 0.67\n852\t      - 0.69\n853\t    - - Pooled (all)\n854\t      - 2000\n855\t      - 0.81\n856\t      - 0.92\n857\t      - 0.74\n858\t      - 0.76\n859\t    - - Leave-one-domain-out\n860\t      - —\n861\t      - 0.74\n862\t      - 0.87\n863\t      - 0.67\n864\t      - 0.69\n865\t  figures_description:\n866\t  - 'Fig S3: Per-domain entropy_mean drift curves showing parallel slopes across domains'\n867\t  analysis: 'Domain generalization confirms distributional drift is not a Wikipedia\n868\t    artifact. The factual-to- creative ordering is interpretable and provides practitioner\n869\t    guidance: depth estimation is most reliable for factual domains. Four domains\n870\t    with LOGO evaluation directly addresses RC6.\n871\t\n872\t    '\n873\t- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n874\t  plan_id: plan_009\n875\t  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on plan_002 data.\n876\t    Tests depth resolution ceiling for non-paraphrastic chains.\n877\t\n878\t    '\n879\t  expected_results:\n880\t    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.58\n881\t    ordinal_gen0_f1: 0.91\n882\t    ordinal_gen1_f1: 0.65\n883\t    ordinal_gen2_f1: 0.48\n884\t    ordinal_gen3_f1: 0.44\n885\t    ordinal_gen4_f1: 0.46\n886\t    ordinal_mae: 0.62\n887\t    ordinal_spearman_rho: 0.81\n888\t    adjacent_class_confusion_pct: 74\n889\t  result_rationale: '5-class F1=0.58 (within [0.50, 0.65] best range) reflects the\n890\t    resolution ceiling of 7 distributional features. gen-0 remains easy (0.91), gen-1\n891\t    moderate (0.65), but gen-2/3/4 have heavily overlapping feature distributions\n892\t    (F1=0.44-0.48). Drift accumulates monotonically but with diminishing marginal\n893\t    signal per step — the distributional distance between gen-3 and gen-4 is smaller\n894\t    than between gen-0 and gen-1 because the generation process approaches a distributional\n895\t    attractor. However, the high Spearman ρ (0.81) and the 74% adjacent-class error\n896\t    pattern confirm that the classifier preserves ordinal structure: mistakes are\n897\t    off-by-one, not off-by-three. This supports ordinal regression and directly addresses\n898\t    RC3: non-paraphrastic chains support at least 3 clean classes (vs ~2 for paraphrasing),\n899\t    and the ordinal signal extends to 5 classes even if exact classification weakens.\n900\t    For data curation, coarse buckets (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) are more useful anyway.\n901\t\n902\t    '\n903\t  tables:\n904\t  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification'\n905\t    columns:\n906\t    - Class\n907\t    - Precision\n908\t    - Recall\n909\t    - F1\n910\t    rows:\n911\t    - - gen-0\n912\t      - 0.92\n913\t      - 0.9\n914\t      - 0.91\n915\t    - - gen-1\n916\t      - 0.63\n917\t      - 0.67\n918\t      - 0.65\n919\t    - - gen-2\n920\t      - 0.46\n921\t      - 0.5\n922\t      - 0.48\n923\t    - - gen-3\n924\t      - 0.43\n925\t      - 0.45\n926\t      - 0.44\n927\t    - - gen-4\n928\t      - 0.48\n929\t      - 0.44\n930\t      - 0.46\n931\t    - - Macro\n932\t      - —\n933\t      - —\n934\t      - 0.58\n935\t  - name: 'Table 13b: Ordinal Error Statistics'\n936\t    columns:\n937\t    - Metric\n938\t    - Value\n939\t    rows:\n940\t    - - MAE\n941\t      - 0.62\n942\t    - - Spearman ρ (pred vs true)\n943\t      - 0.81\n944\t    - - Adjacent-class confusion\n945\t      - 74%\n946\t  figures_description:\n947\t  - 'Fig S4: 5×5 confusion matrix showing off-diagonal mass concentrated on adjacent\n948\t    classes'\n949\t  analysis: '5-class F1=0.58 characterizes the practical resolution ceiling. The ordinal\n950\t    structure (ρ=0.81) is the more important metric than exact accuracy — for curation,\n951\t    knowing approximate depth is more useful than exact classification. This result\n952\t    differentiates from attractor theory''s ~2-class ceiling for paraphrasing.\n953\t\n954\t    '\n955\t- name: human_edit_robustness\n956\t  plan_id: plan_010\n957\t  description: 'Robustness to simulated edits: random word replacement (5/10/20%)\n958\t    and LLM-simulated light editing.\n959\t\n960\t    '\n961\t  expected_results:\n962\t    no_edit_macro_f1: 0.83\n963\t    random_5pct_f1: 0.8\n964\t    random_10pct_f1: 0.75\n965\t    random_20pct_f1: 0.63\n966\t    llm_light_edit_f1: 0.73\n967\t    degradation_slope_per_10pct: -0.1\n968\t  result_rationale: '5% random replacement has minimal impact (Δ=-0.03) because uniform\n969\t    token noise preserves relative distributional differences between depth classes.\n970\t    At 10% (0.75, Δ=-0.08), noise begins masking depth-specific features as random\n971\t    words inject unpredictable NLL values. At 20% (0.63, Δ=-0.20), substantial corruption\n972\t    disrupts the fingerprint significantly but stays above chance. LLM light editing\n973\t    (0.73, comparable to 10% random) changes surface statistics but largely preserves\n974\t    distributional structure — edited text occupies similar reference-model feature\n975\t    space because rephrasing doesn''t fundamentally change what the text is about.\n976\t    The ~linear degradation (-0.10 F1 per 10% edit rate) provides a practitioner rule-of-thumb\n977\t    for estimating signal reliability at different editing intensities. These results\n978\t    address RC5: the method is viable for lightly edited synthetic text (the common\n979\t    case in web data).\n980\t\n981\t    '\n982\t  tables:\n983\t  - name: 'Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits'\n984\t    columns:\n985\t    - Edit Type\n986\t    - Edit Rate\n987\t    - Macro-F1\n988\t    - Δ from Baseline\n989\t    rows:\n990\t    - - None\n991\t      - 0%\n992\t      - 0.83\n993\t      - —\n994\t    - - Random word replacement\n995\t      - 5%\n996\t      - 0.8\n997\t      - '-0.03'\n998\t    - - Random word replacement\n999\t      - 10%\n1000\t      - 0.75\n1001\t      - '-0.08'\n1002\t    - - Random word replacement\n1003\t      - 20%\n1004\t      - 0.63\n1005\t      - '-0.20'\n1006\t    - - LLM light editing\n1007\t      - ~8-12%\n1008\t      - 0.73\n1009\t      - '-0.10'\n1010\t  figures_description:\n1011\t  - 'Fig S5: F1 vs edit rate with linear fit, showing approximately linear degradation'\n1012\t  analysis: 'Practical takeaway: depth estimation remains usable (F1>0.70) at realistic\n1013\t    editing levels (≤10%). Heavy editing (20%+) significantly degrades performance\n1014\t    — an honest limitation. The method targets unedited or lightly post-processed\n1015\t    synthetic text, which is the majority of web-scraped LLM output.\n1016\t\n1017\t    '\n1018\t- name: ld_score_feature\n1019\t  plan_id: plan_011\n1020\t  description: 'Add LD-Score as feature. Pre-registered prediction (RC7): LD-Score\n1021\t    is a level feature exhibiting H2 drift.\n1022\t\n1023\t    '\n1024\t  expected_results:\n1025\t    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n1026\t    ld_score_only_f1: 0.55\n1027\t    ld_score_permutation_importance: 0.05\n1028\t    ld_score_depth_rho: -0.52\n1029\t    ld_score_h2_confirmed: true\n1030\t    full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta: 0.01\n1031\t  result_rationale: 'LD-Score (character-level distributional signature) exhibits\n1032\t    H2 drift (ρ=-0.52) confirming the pre-registered prediction: it is a level feature\n1033\t    sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts that accumulate with depth.\n1034\t    However, adding it to the full feature set gives only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01)\n1035\t    because its signal is largely redundant with surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio)\n1036\t    — both measure vocabulary impoverishment at different granularities. LD-Score\n1037\t    alone (F1=0.55) is weaker than surface features alone (0.76) because character-level\n1038\t    statistics are noisier proxies for depth than word-level statistics. The pre-registered\n1039\t    prediction being confirmed is a small but clean validation of the level/shape\n1040\t    framework: we predicted LD-Score''s behavior class before observing it, and the\n1041\t    prediction held. This belongs in supplementary material.\n1042\t\n1043\t    '\n1044\t  tables:\n1045\t  - name: 'Table S1: LD-Score Feature Integration'\n1046\t    columns:\n1047\t    - Feature Set\n1048\t    - Macro-F1\n1049\t    - Δ from Full\n1050\t    - LD-Score ρ with Depth\n1051\t    rows:\n1052\t    - - Full (7 features)\n1053\t      - 0.82\n1054\t      - —\n1055\t      - —\n1056\t    - - Full + LD-Score\n1057\t      - 0.83\n1058\t      - '+0.01'\n1059\t      - —\n1060\t    - - LD-Score only\n1061\t      - 0.55\n1062\t      - '-0.27'\n1063\t      - -0.52 (H2 confirmed)\n1064\t  figures_description:\n1065\t  - 'Fig S6: LD-Score vs depth showing monotonic decline, confirming pre-registered\n1066\t    H2 prediction'\n1067\t  analysis: 'The pre-registered prediction strengthens the paper''s credibility. LD-Score\n1068\t    drifts as a level feature, exactly as the framework predicts. But its practical\n1069\t    value is minimal — the existing 7 features capture the same signal more efficiently.\n1070\t    This is a useful negative-ish result that reinforces the sufficiency of the current\n1071\t    feature set.\n1072\t\n1073\t    '\n1074\t- name: end_to_end_classifier\n1075\t  plan_id: plan_012\n1076\t  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3-0.6B-Base for end-to-end depth classification.\n1077\t    Compares learned representations vs handcrafted distributional features.\n1078\t\n1079\t    '\n1080\t  expected_results:\n1081\t    qlora_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.8\n1082\t    qlora_gen0_f1: 0.93\n1083\t    qlora_gen1_f1: 0.74\n1084\t    qlora_gen2plus_f1: 0.75\n1085\t    handcrafted_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.83\n1086\t    qlora_cross_gen_logo_f1: 0.66\n1087\t    handcrafted_cross_gen_logo_f1: 0.74\n1088\t    qlora_training_gpu_hours: 14\n1089\t    handcrafted_training_minutes: 3\n1090\t  result_rationale: 'The QLoRA model achieves competitive in-distribution F1 (0.80)\n1091\t    because transformers can implicitly learn distributional features from raw text.\n1092\t    However, it underperforms handcrafted features (0.83) for two reasons: (1) handcrafted\n1093\t    features distill the reference model''s perspective into compact statistics that\n1094\t    directly measure drift, while QLoRA must learn this implicitly; (2) XGBoost on\n1095\t    7 features is harder to overfit than a 0.6B model on ~24K examples. The larger\n1096\t    cross-gen gap (0.66 vs 0.74) reveals that QLoRA memorizes generator-specific text\n1097\t    patterns more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to measure generator-agnostic\n1098\t    distributional drift. The 4000× training cost difference (14 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes)\n1099\t    makes the feature-based approach clearly preferable. This validates the paper''s\n1100\t    focus on interpretable features over end-to-end learning and directly answers\n1101\t    \"why not just fine-tune a model?\"\n1102\t\n1103\t    '\n1104\t  tables:\n1105\t  - name: 'Table S2: Feature-Based vs End-to-End Comparison'\n1106\t    columns:\n1107\t    - Method\n1108\t    - In-Dist F1\n1109\t    - Cross-Gen LOGO F1\n1110\t    - Training Cost\n1111\t    rows:\n1112\t    - - XGBoost + handcrafted\n1113\t      - 0.83\n1114\t      - 0.74\n1115\t      - 3 min\n1116\t    - - QLoRA Qwen3-0.6B\n1117\t      - 0.8\n1118\t      - 0.66\n1119\t      - 14 GPU·h\n1120\t    - - Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\n1121\t      - '+0.03'\n1122\t      - '+0.08'\n1123\t      - —\n1124\t  figures_description: []\n1125\t  analysis: 'Handcrafted features outperform, generalize better, and are 4000× cheaper.\n1126\t    The cross-gen gap (0.08 vs 0.03 in-dist) is the key diagnostic: QLoRA takes generator-specific\n1127\t    shortcuts. This reinforces the contribution positioning — the novelty is the problem\n1128\t    formulation and empirical finding, not the classifier architecture.\n1129\t\n1130\t    '\n1131\t- name: text_length_control\n1132\t  plan_id: plan_013\n1133\t  description: 'Address length confound (RC10): (A) Length distribution analysis by\n1134\t    depth. (B) Length-normalized surface features (TTR, hapax on fixed 200-token windows).\n1135\t    (C) Length-matched subsample comparison.\n1136\t\n1137\t    '\n1138\t  expected_results:\n1139\t    mean_length_gen0_tokens: 285\n1140\t    mean_length_gen1_tokens: 268\n1141\t    mean_length_gen2_tokens: 253\n1142\t    mean_length_gen3_tokens: 244\n1143\t    mean_length_gen4_tokens: 238\n1144\t    length_depth_spearman: -0.35\n1145\t    length_controlled_full_f1: 0.8\n1146\t    length_controlled_surface_only_f1: 0.74\n1147\t    original_full_f1: 0.82\n1148\t    original_surface_only_f1: 0.76\n1149\t    length_controlled_f1_delta_full: -0.02\n1150\t    length_controlled_f1_delta_surface: -0.02\n1151\t    length_normalized_ttr_depth_rho: -0.41\n1152\t    original_ttr_depth_rho: -0.48\n1153\t    length_matched_full_f1: 0.81\n1154\t  result_rationale: 'Text length decreases modestly with depth (285→238 tokens, ρ=-0.35)\n1155\t    because generators tend to produce slightly shorter continuations at deeper steps\n1156\t    due to vocabulary impoverishment and repetition. TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically\n1157\t    length-dependent (shorter texts have higher TTR), which could inflate surface\n1158\t    feature depth signal. However, length-controlled analysis shows minimal F1 drop:\n1159\t    full features decline only 0.02 (0.82→0.80, within [-0.03, 0.0] best range) and\n1160\t    surface features also drop only 0.02 (0.76→0.74). This confirms that surface features\n1161\t    are tracking genuine vocabulary impoverishment, not length artifacts. Length-normalized\n1162\t    TTR still shows significant depth correlation (ρ=-0.41 vs original -0.48), meaning\n1163\t    ~85% of the signal survives length control. Length-matched subsampling (equalizing\n1164\t    length distributions across depths) yields F1=0.81, confirming the classifier\n1165\t    is not exploiting length as a shortcut. This is a critical confound control that\n1166\t    must appear in the main paper — reviewers will look for it.\n1167\t\n1168\t    '\n1169\t  tables:\n1170\t  - name: 'Table S3: Text Length Distribution by Depth'\n1171\t    columns:\n1172\t    - Depth\n1173\t    - Mean Length (tokens)\n1174\t    - Std\n1175\t    - Median\n1176\t    rows:\n1177\t    - - gen-0\n1178\t      - 285\n1179\t      - 82\n1180\t      - 271\n1181\t    - - gen-1\n1182\t      - 268\n1183\t      - 75\n1184\t      - 254\n1185\t    - - gen-2\n1186\t      - 253\n1187\t      - 71\n1188\t      - 240\n1189\t    - - gen-3\n1190\t      - 244\n1191\t      - 68\n1192\t      - 232\n1193\t    - - gen-4\n1194\t      - 238\n1195\t      - 66\n1196\t      - 226\n1197\t  - name: 'Table S4: Length-Controlled Feature Evaluation'\n1198\t    columns:\n1199\t    - Condition\n1200\t    - Full F1\n1201\t    - Surface F1\n1202\t    - Δ Full\n1203\t    - Δ Surface\n1204\t    rows:\n1205\t    - - Original\n1206\t      - 0.82\n1207\t      - 0.76\n1208\t      - —\n1209\t      - —\n1210\t    - - Length-normalized (200-token window)\n1211\t      - 0.8\n1212\t      - 0.74\n1213\t      - '-0.02'\n1214\t      - '-0.02'\n1215\t    - - Length-matched subsample\n1216\t      - 0.81\n1217\t      - 0.75\n1218\t      - '-0.01'\n1219\t      - '-0.01'\n1220\t  - name: 'Table S4b: Length-Controlled Feature Depth Correlation'\n1221\t    columns:\n1222\t    - Feature\n1223\t    - Original ρ\n1224\t    - Length-Normalized ρ\n1225\t    - Signal Retained\n1226\t    rows:\n1227\t    - - ttr\n1228\t      - -0.48\n1229\t      - -0.41\n1230\t      - 85%\n1231\t    - - hapax_ratio\n1232\t      - -0.44\n1233\t      - -0.38\n1234\t      - 86%\n1235\t    - - entropy_mean\n1236\t      - -0.72\n1237\t      - -0.71\n1238\t      - 99%\n1239\t  figures_description:\n1240\t  - 'Fig S7: Length distribution boxplots by depth, showing modest but real length\n1241\t    decline'\n1242\t  - 'Fig S7b: TTR vs depth before and after length normalization — both show significant\n1243\t    negative trend'\n1244\t  analysis: 'Length confound is real but small: ~15% of surface feature signal is\n1245\t    length-driven, ~85% is genuine depth signal. Reference features (entropy_mean)\n1246\t    are essentially unaffected (99% signal retained) because NLL statistics are length-invariant.\n1247\t    The -0.02 F1 delta for both full and surface features confirms the confound doesn''t\n1248\t    drive the main results. This must be in the paper to preempt RC10 and maintain\n1249\t    credibility.\n1250\t\n1251\t    '\n1252\t- name: large_scale_generator\n1253\t  plan_id: plan_014\n1254\t  description: 'Add Qwen3-8B-Base (or similar 7B+ model) to test scale generalization.\n1255\t    1000 seeds × 5 depths on RTX 5090 (32GB). Addresses RC9.\n1256\t\n1257\t    '\n1258\t  expected_results:\n1259\t    qwen3_8b_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.78\n1260\t    qwen3_8b_gen0_f1: 0.91\n1261\t    qwen3_8b_gen1_f1: 0.71\n1262\t    qwen3_8b_gen2plus_f1: 0.73\n1263\t    qwen3_8b_logo_f1: 0.71\n1264\t    qwen3_8b_entropy_mean_depth_rho: -0.58\n1265\t    qwen3_8b_ttr_depth_rho: -0.39\n1266\t    qwen3_8b_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.12\n1267\t    small_generators_mean_drift_per_depth: 0.17\n1268\t    drift_ratio_7b_vs_small: 0.71\n1269\t    h2_confirmed_7b: true\n1270\t    h1_confirmed_7b: true\n1271\t  result_rationale: '7B+ generator in-distribution F1=0.78 and LOGO F1=0.71 (within\n1272\t    [0.65, 0.78] best range) confirm depth signal persists at frontier scale, though\n1273\t    weaker than ≤2.8B generators. The signal attenuation is expected: larger models\n1274\t    produce more fluent, diverse text that compresses depth-driven distributional\n1275\t    drift. Entropy drift per depth step is 0.12 nats vs 0.17 for smaller models (ratio\n1276\t    0.71) — the drift is real but slower. This means deeper chains are needed for\n1277\t    equivalent discriminability at larger scale. Crucially, both H2 (entropy_mean\n1278\t    ρ=-0.58, monotonic drift) and H1 (shape features near-stationary) hold at 8B scale,\n1279\t    confirming dual dynamics generalizes beyond ≤2.8B. The weaker signal is an honest\n1280\t    limitation: \"depth estimation is harder for larger generators that produce more\n1281\t    human-like text.\" But the qualitative pattern is identical — this is strong evidence\n1282\t    that the phenomenon is fundamental to recursive generation, not a small-model\n1283\t    artifact. If anything, the weaker signal at scale makes the finding more interesting:\n1284\t    even frontier models cannot fully escape distributional drift under recursion,\n1285\t    just compress it.\n1286\t\n1287\t    '\n1288\t  tables:\n1289\t  - name: 'Table S5: 7B+ Generator Scale Comparison'\n1290\t    columns:\n1291\t    - Generator\n1292\t    - Params\n1293\t    - In-Dist F1\n1294\t    - LOGO F1\n1295\t    - Drift Rate (nats/depth)\n1296\t    rows:\n1297\t    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n1298\t      - 0.6B\n1299\t      - 0.8\n1300\t      - 0.7\n1301\t      - 0.19\n1302\t    - - Pythia-1B\n1303\t      - 1B\n1304\t      - 0.82\n1305\t      - 0.76\n1306\t      - 0.17\n1307\t    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n1308\t      - 1.5B\n1309\t      - 0.83\n1310\t      - 0.73\n1311\t      - 0.16\n1312\t    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n1313\t      - 1.7B\n1314\t      - 0.82\n1315\t      - 0.72\n1316\t      - 0.16\n1317\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n1318\t      - 2.8B\n1319\t      - 0.84\n1320\t      - 0.77\n1321\t      - 0.15\n1322\t    - - Qwen3-8B-Base\n1323\t      - 8B\n1324\t      - 0.78\n1325\t      - 0.71\n1326\t      - 0.12\n1327\t  - name: 'Table S5b: H1/H2 Verification at 7B+ Scale'\n1328\t    columns:\n1329\t    - Feature\n1330\t    - Depth ρ (8B)\n1331\t    - Depth ρ (≤2.8B mean)\n1332\t    - Classification\n1333\t    rows:\n1334\t    - - entropy_mean\n1335\t      - -0.58\n1336\t      - -0.72\n1337\t      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n1338\t    - - ttr\n1339\t      - -0.39\n1340\t      - -0.48\n1341\t      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n1342\t    - - hapax_ratio\n1343\t      - -0.36\n1344\t      - -0.44\n1345\t      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n1346\t    - - entropy_std\n1347\t      - -0.05\n1348\t      - -0.08\n1349\t      - H1 (stationary)\n1350\t    - - tail_decay\n1351\t      - 0.03\n1352\t      - 0.04\n1353\t      - H1 (stationary)\n1354\t  figures_description:\n1355\t  - 'Fig S8: F1 vs generator scale (log params) showing gentle downward trend with\n1356\t    8B included'\n1357\t  - 'Fig S8b: Entropy drift rate vs scale — drift compresses at larger scale but remains\n1358\t    monotonic'\n1359\t  analysis: 'Scale generalization is confirmed with honest attenuation. The key message:\n1360\t    dual dynamics is scale-invariant in kind (both H1 and H2 hold) but scale-dependent\n1361\t    in magnitude (drift rate compresses with model capacity). This is actually a richer\n1362\t    finding than \"it works the same at all scales\" — it suggests a scaling law for\n1363\t    depth signal that could be explored in future work. For the paper, this transforms\n1364\t    RC9 from a limitation into a nuanced result.'\n1365\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18516c60-8ce6-48b6-8a8e-0e7126e824ef","uuid":"0b918bba-1e8e-4776-b226-865f888f42a2","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:50:55.563Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T221538/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'All three claims fully verified with repositioned framing. Contribution\n  reads as clean empirical science: (1) ordinal depth estimation as new task formulation,\n  (2) dual dynamics discovery (level/diversity drift vs shape stationarity) validated\n  across generator families, reference models, and 7B+ scale — the split is about\n  what features measure, not where they come from, (3) depth-aware curation with OLMo-1B\n  downstream gains significant under multi-seed CIs. Paper anchored in OOD detection\n  + model collapse + data quality literatures (30+ refs). Text length confound controlled\n  — surface features track depth, not length. AAAI/EMNLP accept as strong empirical\n  contribution with surprising finding + immediate utility.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: feature_ablation_and_trend\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Three-part analysis on MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator,\n    Pythia-410M reference). (A) Feature ablation: reference-only vs surface-only vs\n    full. (B) H1/H2 trend analysis per feature. (C) Perplexity pre-validation for\n    Claim 3.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    full_macro_f1: 0.8204\n    surface_only_f1: 0.7628\n    reference_only_f1: 0.7187\n    full_vs_surface_f1_gap: 0.058\n    full_vs_reference_f1_gap: 0.102\n    ref_vs_surf_bootstrap_ci: '[0.706,0.733] vs [0.747,0.774]'\n    h2_features: entropy_mean, ttr, hapax_ratio\n    h1_features: tail_decay, entropy_std, entropy_max, entropy_min\n    entropy_mean_slope_r2: 0.91\n    ttr_slope_r2: 0.88\n    hapax_ratio_slope_r2: 0.85\n    entropy_std_slope_r2: 0.12\n    tail_decay_slope_r2: 0.09\n    median_ppl_by_depth:\n    - 13.5\n    - 4.96\n    - 3.0\n    - 2.07\n    - 1.72\n    ppl_depth_spearman: -0.97\n  result_rationale: '[ACTUAL — plan-001-fix completed] Full > surface > reference\n    ablation gap confirmed. Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) are stronger than\n    initially expected (0.76 vs prior dream''s 0.63) because vocabulary impoverishment\n    is a robust, high-signal depth cue. Reference features alone achieve 0.72 — weaker\n    than surface alone, overturning the naive expectation that reference features\n    dominate. But their complementary value is clear: full set (0.82) significantly\n    exceeds both subsets. The key insight is that entropy_mean (reference-derived)\n    is an H2/drift feature while entropy_std/max/min (also reference-derived) are\n    H1/stationary — the split is about what''s measured (level vs shape), not the\n    feature source. Perplexity monotonically decreases with depth (13.5→1.7) confirming\n    that deeper text is progressively more predictable under the reference model —\n    strong pre-validation for Claim 3''s downstream utility hypothesis.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature Set\n    - Features\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    - CV std\n    rows:\n    - - Reference-only\n      - entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.021\n    - - Surface-only\n      - ttr, hapax_ratio\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.019\n    - - Full\n      - All 7 features\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.013\n  - name: 'Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Trend Analysis (linear regression slope per depth)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Slope\n    - R²\n    - Category\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - -0.18\n      - 0.91\n      - H2-drift\n      - Reference-model surprise decreases monotonically\n    - - ttr\n      - -0.024\n      - 0.88\n      - H2-drift\n      - Lexical diversity declines with depth\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - -0.019\n      - 0.85\n      - H2-drift\n      - Singleton word ratio drops\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.003\n      - 0.12\n      - H1-stationary\n      - Surprise variance near-constant\n    - - entropy_max\n      - 0.008\n      - 0.15\n      - H1-stationary\n      - Tail surprise stable\n    - - entropy_min\n      - -0.005\n      - 0.11\n      - H1-stationary\n      - Floor surprise stable\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.002\n      - 0.09\n      - H1-stationary\n      - Distributional shape robust\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 2a: Feature trend plots (mean ± std) across depth 0-4, showing clear H2 monotonic\n    drift for entropy_mean/ttr/hapax_ratio and H1 stationarity for entropy_std/max/min/tail_decay'\n  - 'Fig 2b: Perplexity by depth (median + IQR), monotonic decrease from 13.5 to 1.7'\n  analysis: 'The ablation gap is central to the paper''s contribution positioning.\n    The full_vs_surface gap (0.058) and full_vs_reference gap (0.102) confirm that\n    both feature families carry non-redundant signal. Critically, entropy_mean is\n    reference-derived but drifts (H2) — this cross-cuts the surface/reference boundary\n    and is the headline finding. The H1/H2 split is clean: R²≥0.85 for drift features,\n    R²≤0.15 for stationary features. This is the empirical backbone of Claim 2.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: '5 generators: Pythia-2.8B, Pythia-1B, OLMo-2-0425-1B, Qwen3-1.7B-Base,\n    Qwen3-0.6B-Base. 1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out\n    (LOGO) CV. Pythia-410M as reference model for all.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    logo_macro_f1_mean: 0.74\n    logo_macro_f1_std: 0.03\n    logo_gen0_f1_mean: 0.87\n    logo_gen1_f1_mean: 0.68\n    logo_gen2plus_f1_mean: 0.7\n    in_distribution_pooled_macro_f1: 0.83\n    pythia_2_8b_held_out_f1: 0.77\n    pythia_1b_held_out_f1: 0.76\n    olmo2_held_out_f1: 0.73\n    qwen3_1_7b_held_out_f1: 0.72\n    qwen3_0_6b_held_out_f1: 0.7\n    leave_one_family_out_pythia_f1: 0.71\n    leave_one_family_out_qwen_f1: 0.74\n    total_chains: 23800\n    attrition_rate_pct: 4.8\n  result_rationale: 'LOGO mean 0.74 (within [0.70, 0.78] best range) reflects cross-generator\n    generalization grounded in actual plan-001 performance (0.82 in-dist). The ~0.09\n    drop from in-distribution to LOGO is expected: each generator has its own distributional\n    signature, and training without the held-out generator forces reliance on transfer\n    of the depth-drift pattern. Pythia-family generators transfer best to each other\n    (0.76-0.77) due to shared pretraining distribution (The Pile). Cross-family transfer\n    (Pythia→Qwen, Pythia→OLMo) drops to 0.70-0.73 because the reference model (Pythia-410M)\n    has different baseline expectations for text from non-Pythia generators, adding\n    noise to features. Qwen3-0.6B-Base is hardest (0.70) because its small capacity\n    produces lower-quality text that drifts faster at gen-1, making the depth ordering\n    harder to predict from larger-model-trained classifiers. Leave-one-family-out\n    for Pythia (train on OLMo+Qwen, test on Pythia-2.8B+1B) gives 0.71, confirming\n    the signal is not Pythia-specific. Attrition slightly higher (4.8%) due to Qwen\n    models'' less stable generation at depth 4.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation'\n    columns:\n    - Held-out Generator\n    - Family\n    - Params\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - Pythia\n      - 2.8B\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.72\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - Pythia\n      - 1B\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.71\n    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n      - OLMo\n      - 1.5B\n      - 0.73\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.68\n    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n      - Qwen\n      - 1.7B\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.67\n    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n      - Qwen\n      - 0.6B\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.65\n    - - Mean ± std\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.74 ± 0.03\n      - 0.87 ± 0.03\n      - 0.68 ± 0.03\n      - 0.70 ± 0.03\n  - name: 'Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV'\n    columns:\n    - Generator\n    - Macro-F1\n    - CV std\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.014\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.016\n    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.015\n    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.017\n    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.02\n    - - Pooled (all 5)\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.012\n  - name: 'Table 4b: Leave-One-Family-Out'\n    columns:\n    - Held-out Family\n    - Generators Held Out\n    - Macro-F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia\n      - Pythia-2.8B + Pythia-1B\n      - 0.71\n    - - Qwen\n      - Qwen3-1.7B + Qwen3-0.6B\n      - 0.74\n    - - OLMo\n      - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n      - 0.73\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 3a: LOGO F1 bar chart by held-out generator, showing Pythia > OLMo > Qwen\n    ordering'\n  - 'Fig 3b: Confusion matrix heatmap for pooled LOGO evaluation showing gen-0 well-separated,\n    gen-1/gen-2+ boundary as main error source'\n  analysis: 'LOGO F1=0.74 is the centerpiece of Claim 1. The ≥0.70 threshold is cleared\n    with margin. Pythia-family slight advantage (0.76-0.77) is a limitation to discuss\n    honestly: Pythia-410M reference model shares tokenizer/architecture with generators.\n    Leave-one-family-out (0.71) still passes the 0.70 threshold, confirming depth\n    signal is generation-process-intrinsic, not family-artifact. The gen-1 vs gen-2+\n    boundary remains the classification bottleneck across all generators.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_family_reference_model\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B,\n    Qwen3-0.6B-Base) on plan_002 data. Key falsification test: does entropy_mean H2\n    drift persist with non-Pythia reference? If not, dual dynamics is Pythia-specific\n    artifact.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1: 0.83\n    olmo2_1b_ref_macro_f1: 0.77\n    qwen3_06b_ref_macro_f1: 0.74\n    entropy_mean_depth_rho_pythia: -0.72\n    entropy_mean_depth_rho_olmo: -0.65\n    entropy_mean_depth_rho_qwen: -0.58\n    cross_ref_mean_rho: -0.65\n    entropy_std_depth_rho_pythia: -0.08\n    entropy_std_depth_rho_olmo: -0.11\n    entropy_std_depth_rho_qwen: -0.06\n    feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean: 0.79\n    feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean: 0.74\n    h2_drift_confirmed_all_refs: true\n    h1_stationarity_confirmed_all_refs: true\n    ensemble_3refs_macro_f1: 0.85\n  result_rationale: 'entropy_mean ρ with depth ranges from -0.58 to -0.72 across reference\n    models (mean -0.65, within [-0.75, -0.55] best range), confirming H2 drift is\n    generation-intrinsic, not Pythia-specific. All three reference models detect monotonic\n    entropy_mean decrease with depth because any well-trained LM assigns progressively\n    higher probability (lower NLL) to deeper text — the text literally becomes more\n    generic/predictable as generation recurses. Pythia-410M gives strongest signal\n    (-0.72) because it was optimized for this pipeline and its 410M capacity is well-calibrated\n    to detect drift (large enough to model human text, small enough to be \"surprised\"\n    by generator deviations). OLMo (-0.65) and Qwen (-0.58) are noisier because their\n    different pretraining distributions create different baselines. Crucially, entropy_std\n    remains near-zero correlation with depth across all three models (|ρ|<0.12), confirming\n    shape stationarity (H1) is also reference-model-agnostic. The dual dynamics is\n    a property of the recursive generation process, not the measurement instrument.\n    This is the key falsification test for Claim 2 — it passes.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)'\n    columns:\n    - Reference Model\n    - Family\n    - Params\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-410M\n      - Pythia\n      - 410M\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.79\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - OLMo\n      - 1B\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.72\n    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n      - Qwen\n      - 0.6B\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.69\n    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n      - Mixed\n      - —\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.81\n  - name: 'Table 6: Entropy Feature Depth Correlation (Spearman ρ) by Reference Model'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Pythia-410M\n    - OLMo-2-1B\n    - Qwen3-0.6B\n    - H1/H2 Classification\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - -0.72\n      - -0.65\n      - -0.58\n      - H2-drift\n    - - entropy_std\n      - -0.08\n      - -0.11\n      - -0.06\n      - H1-stationary\n    - - entropy_max\n      - 0.05\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.07\n      - H1-stationary\n    - - entropy_min\n      - -0.1\n      - -0.07\n      - -0.09\n      - H1-stationary\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.03\n      - H1-stationary\n  - name: 'Table 6b: Cross-Reference Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Pythia↔OLMo\n    - Pythia↔Qwen\n    - OLMo↔Qwen\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.77\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.73\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.66\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 4: entropy_mean vs depth scatter with regression lines for all 3 reference\n    models — parallel downward slopes confirm H2 drift is reference-model-agnostic'\n  - 'Fig 4b: entropy_std vs depth scatter — flat lines for all 3 reference models\n    confirm H1 stationarity'\n  analysis: 'This experiment eliminates the most dangerous threat to the paper: that\n    dual dynamics is a Pythia artifact. With all three reference models showing consistent\n    H2 drift for entropy_mean (ρ range -0.58 to -0.72) and H1 stationarity for shape\n    features (|ρ|<0.12), the level/shape framework is confirmed as generation-intrinsic.\n    The 3-ref ensemble (0.85) exceeding any single ref confirms that diverse reference\n    perspectives capture complementary drift information. For the paper, Table 6 is\n    the most important evidence for the \"what it measures, not where it comes from\"\n    argument.\n\n    '\n- name: downstream_utility\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Fine-tune OLMo-1B (avoiding Pythia-410M circularity) on depth-stratified\n    subsets. ≥3 random seeds with bootstrap CIs. Compare depth-aware filtering vs\n    FastDetectGPT and LD-Score binary baselines. Sample-count-matched comparison to\n    control data size confound.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    downstream_model: OLMo-1B\n    n_seeds: 5\n    ppl_gen0_only_mean: 31.2\n    ppl_gen0_only_ci:\n    - 30.4\n    - 32.0\n    ppl_gen1_only_mean: 33.8\n    ppl_gen1_only_ci:\n    - 32.9\n    - 34.7\n    ppl_gen2_only_mean: 38.5\n    ppl_gen2_only_ci:\n    - 37.2\n    - 39.8\n    ppl_gen3_only_mean: 43.1\n    ppl_gen3_only_ci:\n    - 41.5\n    - 44.7\n    ppl_gen4_only_mean: 47.6\n    ppl_gen4_only_ci:\n    - 45.8\n    - 49.4\n    depth_quality_spearman: 0.74\n    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_mean: 32.1\n    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01_ci:\n    - 31.3\n    - 32.9\n    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_mean: 36.8\n    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt_ci:\n    - 35.6\n    - 38.0\n    ppl_binary_ldscore_mean: 37.2\n    ppl_binary_ldscore_ci:\n    - 35.9\n    - 38.5\n    ppl_no_filter_mean: 40.1\n    depth_filter_vs_binary_improvement_pct: 13.2\n    data_retained_depth_filter_pct: 44\n    data_retained_binary_pct: 30\n    ppl_size_matched_depth_filter: 33.0\n    ppl_size_matched_binary: 37.0\n    size_matched_ci_non_overlapping: true\n  result_rationale: 'OLMo-1B perplexity increases monotonically with training depth\n    (31.2→47.6) because deeper text has compounded distributional drift from human\n    text — fine-tuning on it shifts OLMo-1B''s distribution away from the human test\n    set. The absolute PPL values are higher than the previous dream (which used Pythia-410M)\n    because OLMo-1B is a stronger model with higher baseline expectations. Spearman\n    ρ=0.74 (within [0.65, 0.85]) reflects individual sample variance within depth\n    classes. Depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0+gen-1, PPL=32.1) outperforms binary\n    baselines (~36.8-37.2) by 13.2% (within [8, 20] range) because binary detectors\n    discard all synthetic text — including useful gen-1 content that is distributionally\n    near-human. Binary filters retain only ~30% of data (mostly gen-0) while depth\n    filtering retains 44% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing more and better training data.\n    The size-matched comparison is critical: even when subsampling depth-filtered\n    data to match binary filter count, depth filtering still wins (33.0 vs 37.0, CIs\n    non-overlapping) — confirming the advantage is data quality, not quantity. Using\n    OLMo-1B eliminates the Pythia reference model circularity concern. 5-seed bootstrap\n    CIs are tight enough to establish significance.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Downstream Perplexity by Training Data Depth (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n    columns:\n    - Training Data\n    - PPL Mean\n    - 95% CI\n    - Samples\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0 only\n      - 31.2\n      - '[30.4, 32.0]'\n      - ~4800\n    - - gen-1 only\n      - 33.8\n      - '[32.9, 34.7]'\n      - ~4700\n    - - gen-2 only\n      - 38.5\n      - '[37.2, 39.8]'\n      - ~4400\n    - - gen-3 only\n      - 43.1\n      - '[41.5, 44.7]'\n      - ~4100\n    - - gen-4 only\n      - 47.6\n      - '[45.8, 49.4]'\n      - ~3800\n  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - PPL Mean\n    - 95% CI\n    - Data Retained\n    - Δ vs No-Filter\n    rows:\n    - - Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\n      - 32.1\n      - '[31.3, 32.9]'\n      - 44%\n      - -20.0%\n    - - FastDetectGPT binary\n      - 36.8\n      - '[35.6, 38.0]'\n      - 30%\n      - -8.2%\n    - - LD-Score binary\n      - 37.2\n      - '[35.9, 38.5]'\n      - 28%\n      - -7.2%\n    - - No filtering\n      - 40.1\n      - '[38.8, 41.4]'\n      - 100%\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 8b: Size-Matched Comparison (subsample to equal data count)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Matched Count\n    - PPL Mean\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - Depth-filtered (subsampled)\n      - ~5500\n      - 33.0\n      - '[32.1, 33.9]'\n    - - Binary-filtered\n      - ~5500\n      - 37.0\n      - '[35.8, 38.2]'\n    - - CIs non-overlapping\n      - —\n      - 'YES'\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 5a: Perplexity vs training depth curve with error bars (5 seeds), monotonically\n    increasing'\n  - 'Fig 5b: Bar chart comparing filtering methods with 95% CI error bars, showing\n    depth-aware < binary < no-filter'\n  analysis: 'This is the practical punchline. The 13.2% improvement with CIs non-overlapping\n    under size-matching establishes that depth-aware filtering provides genuine data\n    quality advantage, not just a data quantity effect. gen-1 text is \"good enough\"\n    for training (PPL 33.8 vs gen-0''s 31.2 — 8% gap), but gen-2+ is harmful (PPL\n    38.5+, >23% worse than gen-0). Binary detectors force an all-or-nothing choice\n    and discard useful gen-1 content. This directly validates Claim 3 under the strengthened\n    protocol (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds, CIs, size-matched).\n\n    '\n- name: prompt_type_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n    1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths × 3 prompt types.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    continuation_macro_f1: 0.82\n    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.77\n    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.69\n    continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.17\n    elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.13\n    inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.08\n    cross_prompt_type_macro_f1: 0.71\n    prompt_drift_rate_f1_spearman: 0.93\n  result_rationale: 'Continuation preserves strongest depth signal (0.82) because\n    each step directly extends previous output, maximizing cumulative drift. Elaboration\n    (0.77) injects new semantic direction via prompt instruction, partially resetting\n    drift per step. Inspired rewriting (0.69) provides most semantic freedom — the\n    model can re-anchor to its pretraining distribution at each step, yielding weakest\n    depth accumulation. The strong correlation between per-depth entropy drift rate\n    and F1 (ρ=0.93) confirms the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation\n    → faster drift accumulation → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt transfer (0.71)\n    is moderate because drift signatures are partially shared but rate differences\n    create distribution shift. This result enriches the theoretical story without\n    changing the core claims.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type (Pythia-2.8B)'\n    columns:\n    - Prompt Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    - Drift Rate (nats/depth)\n    rows:\n    - - Continuation\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.17\n    - - Elaboration\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.13\n    - - Inspired rewriting\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.08\n    - - Cross-prompt LOGO\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.65\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S1: Entropy drift trajectory per prompt type, showing separation increasing\n    with depth'\n  analysis: 'The ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns\n    with theory: semantic coupling determines drift accumulation rate. This is a supporting\n    result — it enriches the paper but isn''t strictly necessary for the core claims.\n    Best used to show that depth signal is graded and mechanism-consistent.\n\n    '\n- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 5 depths)\n    vs continuation chains. Direct test of attractor convergence (H1) vs monotonic\n    divergence (H2).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    paraphrase_macro_f1_3class: 0.55\n    paraphrase_gen0_f1: 0.79\n    paraphrase_gen1_f1: 0.43\n    paraphrase_gen2plus_f1: 0.44\n    continuation_macro_f1_3class: 0.82\n    paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.05\n    continuation_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.48\n    paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct: 64\n    ks_statistic_para_vs_cont_depth3: 0.39\n    ks_p_value: 1.0e-05\n  result_rationale: 'Paraphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.55) because\n    the semantic-preservation constraint creates attractor dynamics — distributional\n    features converge after ~2 steps, consistent with ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory.\n    By depth 3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.05 nats) while\n    continuation drift continues (0.48 nats). The 64% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic\n    chains (depth d closer to d-2 than d-1 in feature space) directly corroborates\n    2-period attractor cycle predictions. The KS test (D=0.39, p<0.001) provides formal\n    evidence that paraphrastic and continuation depth-3 distributions are distinct.\n    This elevates the paper from \"a depth classifier\" to \"an empirical test of generation\n    dynamics\" by connecting to established theoretical literature. Our H2 divergence\n    for non-paraphrastic chains and H1 convergence for paraphrastic chains are two\n    sides of the same coin — the presence or absence of semantic constraint determines\n    whether drift accumulates or converges.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Dynamics'\n    columns:\n    - Chain Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Entropy Drift @depth3\n    - Cycle Detection %\n    - KS D\n    rows:\n    - - Continuation\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.48 nats\n      - <5%\n      - —\n    - - Paraphrasing\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.05 nats\n      - 64%\n      - —\n    - - KS test (depth-3)\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.39 (p<0.001)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 6: Dual panel — (left) entropy_mean trajectory for continuation (monotonic\n    decline) vs paraphrasing (plateau after depth 2); (right) feature space PCA showing\n    continuation chains diverging, paraphrastic chains oscillating'\n  analysis: 'This is the conceptual anchor connecting our empirical finding to the\n    attractor cycle literature. The clean separation validates why we focus on non-paraphrastic\n    chains and why our approach succeeds where paraphrasing-based analysis would fail\n    at depth estimation beyond ~2 classes.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_model_chains\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Cross-model chains: different generator per depth step (randomly sampled\n    from 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Tests ecological validity — real contamination\n    uses diverse generators.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    cross_model_macro_f1: 0.71\n    single_model_macro_f1_mean: 0.82\n    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.88\n    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.64\n    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.63\n    cross_vs_single_delta: -0.11\n    cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.12\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains lose ~11 points from single-model (0.71 vs\n    0.82) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities at\n    each step — the new generator''s idiosyncratic signature partially masks the accumulated\n    depth drift. However, F1=0.71 still far exceeds chance (0.33) because the underlying\n    drift phenomenon persists: regardless of which generator produces each step, cumulative\n    deviation from human text distribution still increases with depth. gen-0 remains\n    well-separated (0.88) — human text is fundamentally distinct from any generator.\n    The gen-1/gen-2+ boundary degrades most (0.64/0.63) because generator-switching\n    noise creates feature-space overlap between these classes. Entropy drift rate\n    (0.12 nats/depth) is lower than single-model (0.17) because each generator transition\n    partially resets the drift trajectory. This is the honest cost of ecological validity\n    — reported transparently, it strengthens the paper.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Chain Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Single-model (mean of 5)\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.78\n    - - Cross-model (random switch)\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.63\n    - - Δ\n      - -0.11\n      - -0.06\n      - -0.12\n      - -0.15\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S2: Feature space scatter (PCA) for single-model vs cross-model chains showing\n    increased class overlap under generator switching'\n  analysis: 'The 11-point drop is expected and honestly bounded. For data curation,\n    the key utility is still maintained: the method separates human from synthetic\n    (gen-0 F1=0.88) even under realistic multi-generator contamination. Finer depth\n    estimation within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades — an honest limitation\n    for the Discussion section.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_domain_seeds\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: '4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC), creative\n    (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 generators × 5 depths.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.83\n    news_macro_f1: 0.8\n    scientific_macro_f1: 0.78\n    creative_macro_f1: 0.74\n    cross_domain_logo_f1: 0.74\n    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.81\n    domain_f1_variance: 0.0012\n  result_rationale: 'Wikipedia performs best (0.83) as it matches the MVP development\n    setting with structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to faithfully\n    continue. News (0.80) has similar factual density. Scientific text (0.78) has\n    specialized vocabulary that partially masks depth drift in surface features, though\n    reference features still detect it. Creative writing is hardest (0.74) because\n    generators produce more fluent, human-like creative text — the distributional\n    gap between human and generated creative text is smaller, reducing overall drift\n    magnitude. The low F1 variance across domains (0.0012) indicates depth signal\n    is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Cross-domain LOGO (0.74) confirms generalization\n    without domain-specific calibration. This addresses RC6 (ecological validity)\n    by showing the approach works across diverse text types.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification'\n    columns:\n    - Domain\n    - Seeds\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Wikipedia\n      - 500\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.79\n    - - News\n      - 500\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.76\n    - - Scientific\n      - 500\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.73\n    - - Creative\n      - 500\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.69\n    - - Pooled (all)\n      - 2000\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.76\n    - - Leave-one-domain-out\n      - —\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.69\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S3: Per-domain entropy_mean drift curves showing parallel slopes across domains'\n  analysis: 'Domain generalization confirms distributional drift is not a Wikipedia\n    artifact. The factual-to- creative ordering is interpretable and provides practitioner\n    guidance: depth estimation is most reliable for factual domains. Four domains\n    with LOGO evaluation directly addresses RC6.\n\n    '\n- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on plan_002 data.\n    Tests depth resolution ceiling for non-paraphrastic chains.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.58\n    ordinal_gen0_f1: 0.91\n    ordinal_gen1_f1: 0.65\n    ordinal_gen2_f1: 0.48\n    ordinal_gen3_f1: 0.44\n    ordinal_gen4_f1: 0.46\n    ordinal_mae: 0.62\n    ordinal_spearman_rho: 0.81\n    adjacent_class_confusion_pct: 74\n  result_rationale: '5-class F1=0.58 (within [0.50, 0.65] best range) reflects the\n    resolution ceiling of 7 distributional features. gen-0 remains easy (0.91), gen-1\n    moderate (0.65), but gen-2/3/4 have heavily overlapping feature distributions\n    (F1=0.44-0.48). Drift accumulates monotonically but with diminishing marginal\n    signal per step — the distributional distance between gen-3 and gen-4 is smaller\n    than between gen-0 and gen-1 because the generation process approaches a distributional\n    attractor. However, the high Spearman ρ (0.81) and the 74% adjacent-class error\n    pattern confirm that the classifier preserves ordinal structure: mistakes are\n    off-by-one, not off-by-three. This supports ordinal regression and directly addresses\n    RC3: non-paraphrastic chains support at least 3 clean classes (vs ~2 for paraphrasing),\n    and the ordinal signal extends to 5 classes even if exact classification weakens.\n    For data curation, coarse buckets (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) are more useful anyway.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification'\n    columns:\n    - Class\n    - Precision\n    - Recall\n    - F1\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.91\n    - - gen-1\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.65\n    - - gen-2\n      - 0.46\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.48\n    - - gen-3\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.45\n      - 0.44\n    - - gen-4\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.46\n    - - Macro\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.58\n  - name: 'Table 13b: Ordinal Error Statistics'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    rows:\n    - - MAE\n      - 0.62\n    - - Spearman ρ (pred vs true)\n      - 0.81\n    - - Adjacent-class confusion\n      - 74%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S4: 5×5 confusion matrix showing off-diagonal mass concentrated on adjacent\n    classes'\n  analysis: '5-class F1=0.58 characterizes the practical resolution ceiling. The ordinal\n    structure (ρ=0.81) is the more important metric than exact accuracy — for curation,\n    knowing approximate depth is more useful than exact classification. This result\n    differentiates from attractor theory''s ~2-class ceiling for paraphrasing.\n\n    '\n- name: human_edit_robustness\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Robustness to simulated edits: random word replacement (5/10/20%)\n    and LLM-simulated light editing.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    no_edit_macro_f1: 0.83\n    random_5pct_f1: 0.8\n    random_10pct_f1: 0.75\n    random_20pct_f1: 0.63\n    llm_light_edit_f1: 0.73\n    degradation_slope_per_10pct: -0.1\n  result_rationale: '5% random replacement has minimal impact (Δ=-0.03) because uniform\n    token noise preserves relative distributional differences between depth classes.\n    At 10% (0.75, Δ=-0.08), noise begins masking depth-specific features as random\n    words inject unpredictable NLL values. At 20% (0.63, Δ=-0.20), substantial corruption\n    disrupts the fingerprint significantly but stays above chance. LLM light editing\n    (0.73, comparable to 10% random) changes surface statistics but largely preserves\n    distributional structure — edited text occupies similar reference-model feature\n    space because rephrasing doesn''t fundamentally change what the text is about.\n    The ~linear degradation (-0.10 F1 per 10% edit rate) provides a practitioner rule-of-thumb\n    for estimating signal reliability at different editing intensities. These results\n    address RC5: the method is viable for lightly edited synthetic text (the common\n    case in web data).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits'\n    columns:\n    - Edit Type\n    - Edit Rate\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Δ from Baseline\n    rows:\n    - - None\n      - 0%\n      - 0.83\n      - —\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 5%\n      - 0.8\n      - '-0.03'\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 10%\n      - 0.75\n      - '-0.08'\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 20%\n      - 0.63\n      - '-0.20'\n    - - LLM light editing\n      - ~8-12%\n      - 0.73\n      - '-0.10'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S5: F1 vs edit rate with linear fit, showing approximately linear degradation'\n  analysis: 'Practical takeaway: depth estimation remains usable (F1>0.70) at realistic\n    editing levels (≤10%). Heavy editing (20%+) significantly degrades performance\n    — an honest limitation. The method targets unedited or lightly post-processed\n    synthetic text, which is the majority of web-scraped LLM output.\n\n    '\n- name: ld_score_feature\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Add LD-Score as feature. Pre-registered prediction (RC7): LD-Score\n    is a level feature exhibiting H2 drift.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n    ld_score_only_f1: 0.55\n    ld_score_permutation_importance: 0.05\n    ld_score_depth_rho: -0.52\n    ld_score_h2_confirmed: true\n    full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta: 0.01\n  result_rationale: 'LD-Score (character-level distributional signature) exhibits\n    H2 drift (ρ=-0.52) confirming the pre-registered prediction: it is a level feature\n    sensitive to vocabulary/character frequency shifts that accumulate with depth.\n    However, adding it to the full feature set gives only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01)\n    because its signal is largely redundant with surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio)\n    — both measure vocabulary impoverishment at different granularities. LD-Score\n    alone (F1=0.55) is weaker than surface features alone (0.76) because character-level\n    statistics are noisier proxies for depth than word-level statistics. The pre-registered\n    prediction being confirmed is a small but clean validation of the level/shape\n    framework: we predicted LD-Score''s behavior class before observing it, and the\n    prediction held. This belongs in supplementary material.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S1: LD-Score Feature Integration'\n    columns:\n    - Feature Set\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Δ from Full\n    - LD-Score ρ with Depth\n    rows:\n    - - Full (7 features)\n      - 0.82\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - Full + LD-Score\n      - 0.83\n      - '+0.01'\n      - —\n    - - LD-Score only\n      - 0.55\n      - '-0.27'\n      - -0.52 (H2 confirmed)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S6: LD-Score vs depth showing monotonic decline, confirming pre-registered\n    H2 prediction'\n  analysis: 'The pre-registered prediction strengthens the paper''s credibility. LD-Score\n    drifts as a level feature, exactly as the framework predicts. But its practical\n    value is minimal — the existing 7 features capture the same signal more efficiently.\n    This is a useful negative-ish result that reinforces the sufficiency of the current\n    feature set.\n\n    '\n- name: end_to_end_classifier\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3-0.6B-Base for end-to-end depth classification.\n    Compares learned representations vs handcrafted distributional features.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qlora_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.8\n    qlora_gen0_f1: 0.93\n    qlora_gen1_f1: 0.74\n    qlora_gen2plus_f1: 0.75\n    handcrafted_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.83\n    qlora_cross_gen_logo_f1: 0.66\n    handcrafted_cross_gen_logo_f1: 0.74\n    qlora_training_gpu_hours: 14\n    handcrafted_training_minutes: 3\n  result_rationale: 'The QLoRA model achieves competitive in-distribution F1 (0.80)\n    because transformers can implicitly learn distributional features from raw text.\n    However, it underperforms handcrafted features (0.83) for two reasons: (1) handcrafted\n    features distill the reference model''s perspective into compact statistics that\n    directly measure drift, while QLoRA must learn this implicitly; (2) XGBoost on\n    7 features is harder to overfit than a 0.6B model on ~24K examples. The larger\n    cross-gen gap (0.66 vs 0.74) reveals that QLoRA memorizes generator-specific text\n    patterns more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to measure generator-agnostic\n    distributional drift. The 4000× training cost difference (14 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes)\n    makes the feature-based approach clearly preferable. This validates the paper''s\n    focus on interpretable features over end-to-end learning and directly answers\n    \"why not just fine-tune a model?\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S2: Feature-Based vs End-to-End Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - In-Dist F1\n    - Cross-Gen LOGO F1\n    - Training Cost\n    rows:\n    - - XGBoost + handcrafted\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.74\n      - 3 min\n    - - QLoRA Qwen3-0.6B\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.66\n      - 14 GPU·h\n    - - Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\n      - '+0.03'\n      - '+0.08'\n      - —\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Handcrafted features outperform, generalize better, and are 4000× cheaper.\n    The cross-gen gap (0.08 vs 0.03 in-dist) is the key diagnostic: QLoRA takes generator-specific\n    shortcuts. This reinforces the contribution positioning — the novelty is the problem\n    formulation and empirical finding, not the classifier architecture.\n\n    '\n- name: text_length_control\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Address length confound (RC10): (A) Length distribution analysis by\n    depth. (B) Length-normalized surface features (TTR, hapax on fixed 200-token windows).\n    (C) Length-matched subsample comparison.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mean_length_gen0_tokens: 285\n    mean_length_gen1_tokens: 268\n    mean_length_gen2_tokens: 253\n    mean_length_gen3_tokens: 244\n    mean_length_gen4_tokens: 238\n    length_depth_spearman: -0.35\n    length_controlled_full_f1: 0.8\n    length_controlled_surface_only_f1: 0.74\n    original_full_f1: 0.82\n    original_surface_only_f1: 0.76\n    length_controlled_f1_delta_full: -0.02\n    length_controlled_f1_delta_surface: -0.02\n    length_normalized_ttr_depth_rho: -0.41\n    original_ttr_depth_rho: -0.48\n    length_matched_full_f1: 0.81\n  result_rationale: 'Text length decreases modestly with depth (285→238 tokens, ρ=-0.35)\n    because generators tend to produce slightly shorter continuations at deeper steps\n    due to vocabulary impoverishment and repetition. TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically\n    length-dependent (shorter texts have higher TTR), which could inflate surface\n    feature depth signal. However, length-controlled analysis shows minimal F1 drop:\n    full features decline only 0.02 (0.82→0.80, within [-0.03, 0.0] best range) and\n    surface features also drop only 0.02 (0.76→0.74). This confirms that surface features\n    are tracking genuine vocabulary impoverishment, not length artifacts. Length-normalized\n    TTR still shows significant depth correlation (ρ=-0.41 vs original -0.48), meaning\n    ~85% of the signal survives length control. Length-matched subsampling (equalizing\n    length distributions across depths) yields F1=0.81, confirming the classifier\n    is not exploiting length as a shortcut. This is a critical confound control that\n    must appear in the main paper — reviewers will look for it.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S3: Text Length Distribution by Depth'\n    columns:\n    - Depth\n    - Mean Length (tokens)\n    - Std\n    - Median\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 285\n      - 82\n      - 271\n    - - gen-1\n      - 268\n      - 75\n      - 254\n    - - gen-2\n      - 253\n      - 71\n      - 240\n    - - gen-3\n      - 244\n      - 68\n      - 232\n    - - gen-4\n      - 238\n      - 66\n      - 226\n  - name: 'Table S4: Length-Controlled Feature Evaluation'\n    columns:\n    - Condition\n    - Full F1\n    - Surface F1\n    - Δ Full\n    - Δ Surface\n    rows:\n    - - Original\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.76\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - Length-normalized (200-token window)\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.74\n      - '-0.02'\n      - '-0.02'\n    - - Length-matched subsample\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.75\n      - '-0.01'\n      - '-0.01'\n  - name: 'Table S4b: Length-Controlled Feature Depth Correlation'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Original ρ\n    - Length-Normalized ρ\n    - Signal Retained\n    rows:\n    - - ttr\n      - -0.48\n      - -0.41\n      - 85%\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - -0.44\n      - -0.38\n      - 86%\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - -0.72\n      - -0.71\n      - 99%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S7: Length distribution boxplots by depth, showing modest but real length\n    decline'\n  - 'Fig S7b: TTR vs depth before and after length normalization — both show significant\n    negative trend'\n  analysis: 'Length confound is real but small: ~15% of surface feature signal is\n    length-driven, ~85% is genuine depth signal. Reference features (entropy_mean)\n    are essentially unaffected (99% signal retained) because NLL statistics are length-invariant.\n    The -0.02 F1 delta for both full and surface features confirms the confound doesn''t\n    drive the main results. This must be in the paper to preempt RC10 and maintain\n    credibility.\n\n    '\n- name: large_scale_generator\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: 'Add Qwen3-8B-Base (or similar 7B+ model) to test scale generalization.\n    1000 seeds × 5 depths on RTX 5090 (32GB). Addresses RC9.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen3_8b_in_dist_macro_f1: 0.78\n    qwen3_8b_gen0_f1: 0.91\n    qwen3_8b_gen1_f1: 0.71\n    qwen3_8b_gen2plus_f1: 0.73\n    qwen3_8b_logo_f1: 0.71\n    qwen3_8b_entropy_mean_depth_rho: -0.58\n    qwen3_8b_ttr_depth_rho: -0.39\n    qwen3_8b_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.12\n    small_generators_mean_drift_per_depth: 0.17\n    drift_ratio_7b_vs_small: 0.71\n    h2_confirmed_7b: true\n    h1_confirmed_7b: true\n  result_rationale: '7B+ generator in-distribution F1=0.78 and LOGO F1=0.71 (within\n    [0.65, 0.78] best range) confirm depth signal persists at frontier scale, though\n    weaker than ≤2.8B generators. The signal attenuation is expected: larger models\n    produce more fluent, diverse text that compresses depth-driven distributional\n    drift. Entropy drift per depth step is 0.12 nats vs 0.17 for smaller models (ratio\n    0.71) — the drift is real but slower. This means deeper chains are needed for\n    equivalent discriminability at larger scale. Crucially, both H2 (entropy_mean\n    ρ=-0.58, monotonic drift) and H1 (shape features near-stationary) hold at 8B scale,\n    confirming dual dynamics generalizes beyond ≤2.8B. The weaker signal is an honest\n    limitation: \"depth estimation is harder for larger generators that produce more\n    human-like text.\" But the qualitative pattern is identical — this is strong evidence\n    that the phenomenon is fundamental to recursive generation, not a small-model\n    artifact. If anything, the weaker signal at scale makes the finding more interesting:\n    even frontier models cannot fully escape distributional drift under recursion,\n    just compress it.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S5: 7B+ Generator Scale Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Generator\n    - Params\n    - In-Dist F1\n    - LOGO F1\n    - Drift Rate (nats/depth)\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-0.6B-Base\n      - 0.6B\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.19\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - 1B\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.17\n    - - OLMo-2-0425-1B\n      - 1.5B\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.73\n      - 0.16\n    - - Qwen3-1.7B-Base\n      - 1.7B\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.16\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - 2.8B\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.15\n    - - Qwen3-8B-Base\n      - 8B\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.12\n  - name: 'Table S5b: H1/H2 Verification at 7B+ Scale'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Depth ρ (8B)\n    - Depth ρ (≤2.8B mean)\n    - Classification\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - -0.58\n      - -0.72\n      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n    - - ttr\n      - -0.39\n      - -0.48\n      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - -0.36\n      - -0.44\n      - H2 (weaker but confirmed)\n    - - entropy_std\n      - -0.05\n      - -0.08\n      - H1 (stationary)\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.04\n      - H1 (stationary)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S8: F1 vs generator scale (log params) showing gentle downward trend with\n    8B included'\n  - 'Fig S8b: Entropy drift rate vs scale — drift compresses at larger scale but remains\n    monotonic'\n  analysis: 'Scale generalization is confirmed with honest attenuation. The key message:\n    dual dynamics is scale-invariant in kind (both H1 and H2 hold) but scale-dependent\n    in magnitude (drift rate compresses with model capacity). This is actually a richer\n    finding than \"it works the same at all scales\" — it suggests a scaling law for\n    depth signal that could be explored in future work. For the paper, this transforms\n    RC9 from a limitation into a nuanced result.'\n","numLines":1365,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1365}}}
